Based on your DocuTrack Pro system in the smart-file-navigator folder, here are the detailed explanations of the database relationships:
1. One-to-Many: User → Documents (uploaded_by relationship)

This relationship indicates that one user can upload multiple documents, but each document has only one uploader. In your system:

    A single user (client, processing staff, or deputy director) can create and upload numerous documents over time
    Each document record maintains a reference to its uploader through the uploaded_by_id foreign key field
    This relationship is crucial for tracking document ownership, implementing access controls, and generating user-specific reports
    Example: A client user might upload 10 different documents for processing, but each document is linked to that specific client

2. One-to-Many: Document → Document_Versions

This relationship shows that one document can have multiple versions, but each version belongs to only one document. In your implementation:

    Documents undergo revisions during the processing workflow, creating new versions
    Each version maintains the complete content state at a specific point in time
    The document_versions table stores each revision with metadata about who made changes and when
    This enables version control, change tracking, and the ability to revert to previous document states
    Example: A contract document might have 5 versions as it goes through review and approval cycles

3. One-to-Many: User → Notifications (both sender and receiver)

This is actually two separate one-to-many relationships in your system:

As Sender (from_user_id):

    One user can send multiple notifications to different recipients
    System tracks who initiated each notification for audit purposes

As Receiver (to_user_id):

    One user can receive multiple notifications from various sources
    Each user has their own notification inbox with read/unread status

This dual relationship enables comprehensive notification tracking and ensures users can manage their communication history effectively.
4. Many-to-One: Notifications → Documents

This relationship means multiple notifications can relate to the same document, but each notification references only one document. In your system:

    A single document generates various notifications throughout its lifecycle (upload confirmation, status changes, completion alerts, deadline reminders)
    Each notification maintains a reference to the specific document it concerns
    This enables document-centric notification filtering and ensures users receive relevant updates about documents they're involved with
    Example: One document might generate 8 notifications (upload, assignment, in-progress, review, completion, etc.)

5. One-to-Many: Document → Processing_Logs

This relationship indicates that one document has multiple processing log entries, but each log entry belongs to only one document. In your implementation:

    Every action performed on a document creates a log entry for audit trails
    Processing logs capture status changes, edits, assignments, and other workflow events
    This relationship supports compliance requirements, performance analytics, and troubleshooting
    The logs provide a complete history of document processing activities
    Example: A document might have 15 log entries tracking its journey from upload to completion

Service Interconnection Diagram Explanation

The diagram shows the serverless cloud architecture of your DocuTrack Pro system with the following service interconnections:
Frontend (React/S3) Layer

    React Application: Your TypeScript-based user interface components hosted as static files
    Amazon S3 Static Hosting: Serves the compiled React application to users' browsers
    Bidirectional Communication: Frontend sends API requests and receives responses through secure HTTP/HTTPS protocols

API Gateway Layer

    Central API Hub: Acts as the single entry point for all client requests
    Request Routing: Directs incoming requests to appropriate Lambda functions based on endpoints
    Security Enforcement: Handles authentication, rate limiting, and CORS policies
    Response Management: Formats and returns responses to the frontend

Lambda Functions Layer

    Serverless Compute: Individual functions handle specific business logic (authentication, document operations, notifications)
    Event-Driven Processing: Functions execute only when triggered by API requests
    Stateless Operations: Each function call is independent, ensuring scalability
    Cost Efficiency: Pay only for actual compute time used

RDS (MySQL) Database Layer

    Structured Data Storage: Stores user accounts, document metadata, notifications, and processing logs
    ACID Compliance: Ensures data consistency and reliability for critical business operations
    Relational Integrity: Maintains foreign key relationships and data validation
    Backup and Recovery: Automated backups and point-in-time recovery capabilities

S3 Document Storage

    File Repository: Stores actual document files (PDFs, Word documents, text files)
    Scalable Storage: Handles files of various sizes with virtually unlimited capacity
    Security Features: Encryption at rest and in transit, access control policies
    Integration: Lambda functions interact with S3 for file upload, retrieval, and management operations

CloudWatch Monitoring

    System Observability: Monitors all AWS services for performance, errors, and usage metrics
    Log Aggregation: Collects logs from Lambda functions, API Gateway, and other services
    Alerting: Sends notifications when system thresholds are exceeded or errors occur
    Performance Analytics: Provides insights for optimization and capacity planning

Data Flow Pattern

    User Request: Frontend sends request to API Gateway
    Authentication: API Gateway validates JWT tokens
    Function Execution: Lambda function processes business logic
    Database Operations: Lambda interacts with RDS for structured data
    File Operations: Lambda manages documents in S3 storage
    Monitoring: CloudWatch logs all activities and metrics
    Response: Results flow back through the same path to the frontend

This architecture provides high availability, automatic scaling, and cost-effectiveness while maintaining enterprise-grade security and performance standards.